WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.320
Each night, the planes start coming in at 11.
每天晚上11点，飞机开始陆续抵达。

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.000
One after another, a massive airlift of cargo, like you'd see at wartime.
一架接一架，那是你在战时才会看到的、大规模的货运空运。

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.220
You can see, actually there are two runways out there.
事实上你可以看到，外面有两条跑道。

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.480
You've got one on the left and the right.
左边一条，右边一条。

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:15.100
You've got an aircraft fixing to touch down on, looks like on the right.
有一架飞机正准备着陆，看起来是在右边那条。

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:20.700
You've got one coming up right behind it about 15 seconds behind it that's fixing to land on the left.
它后面紧跟着另一架飞机，大约落后15秒，正准备在左边降落。

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:22.700
It's rush hour here at FedEx.
这里是联邦快递的交通晚高峰。

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.740
The planes land every 45 seconds or so for two hours.
飞机每隔45秒左右降落一次，持续两个小时。

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:41.220
130 jets here at the Memphis hub of Federal Express Express, 1.2 million pieces of cargo are unloaded, slid down ramps and conveyor belts, scanned and sorted and rerouted and rescanned by 8,500 people under lights in the middle of the night.
在联邦快递的孟菲斯枢纽，停放着130架喷气式飞机，120万件货物被卸下，顺着坡道和传送带滑下，在半夜的灯光下，由8500名工人进行扫描、分拣、重新路由和再次扫描。

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:48.980
And three hours after they start, the packages are packed onto planes to their destinations, and the airlift out of Memphis begins.
在它们开始卸货三个小时后，这些包裹就被装上飞机运往目的地，从孟菲斯的空运随之展开。

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:50.580
It's frantic.
这里一片忙碌。

00:00:51.760 --> 00:01:02.700
Meanwhile, somewhere, probably sleeping, are hundreds of thousands of Americans in one big, big hurry to get their stuff delivered to them on time in one day.
与此同时，在某个地方，可能正有几十万美国人在睡觉，他们急切地盼望着自己的东西能在一天内按时送到。

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:09.320
In the middle of the Memphis airport, come midnight, we look like one very anxious nation.
午夜时分，在孟菲斯机场的中心，我们看起来就像一个极其焦虑的国家。

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.800
Everywhere around the Federal Express hub there are television screens with two times on them.
联邦快递枢纽周围到处都是显示着两个时间的电视屏幕。

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:20.470
One is the current time.
一个是当前时间。

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.140
The other is the time that the sort will go down.
另一个是分拣结束的时间。

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.230
That is the time that they hope that they will stop taking in new packages.
那是他们希望停止接收新包裹的时间。

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.480
All the planes will be in, all the packages will go in.
所有的飞机都将到达，所有的包裹都将录入。

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:34.900
If everything goes perfectly, good weather, no maintenance problems, the sort goes down at $2.07.
如果一切顺利，天气晴朗，没有维护问题，分拣将在2点07分结束。

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.760
And for every minute they go over $2.07, the cost per minute?
每超出一分钟，每分钟的成本是多少？

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.580
Last I heard, it was over $100,000 a minute.
我听到的最新数字是，每分钟超过10万美元。

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.980
That's employee costs, extra flights to get packages where they'll need to go, refunds to customers whose packages were delayed because the sort went late.
那包括员工成本、将包裹送达所需额外航班的费用、以及因分拣延迟而对延误包裹客户的退款。

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.320
The guy who explained this to me, Ron Nichols, is 31, and he sits in the hot seat himself.
向我解释这一切的人叫罗恩·尼科尔斯（Ron Nichols），今年31岁，他自己就坐在风口浪尖上。

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:00.160
A few nights a week, he is the one who determines when the sort goes down.
一周中有几个晚上，就是由他来决定分拣何时结束。

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:05.260
If a plane with 50,000 packages is going to be late, should he hold the sort?
如果一架载有5万个包裹的飞机将要迟到，他该不该推迟分拣？

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:06.640
How long?
推迟多久？

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.650
In 10 minutes, this 31-year-old can cost Federal Express $1 million.
在10分钟内，这位31岁的年轻人可能会让联邦快递损失100万美元。

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:14.840
He likes the pressure.
他很享受这种压力。

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:15.880
It's a nirvana.
这是一种涅槃。

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.700
Almost around 145, you get an ultimate enlightenment because you see everything.
差不多在1点45左右，你会获得一种终极的顿悟，因为你能看清一切。

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.120
You see the big picture, exactly what's going on everywhere.
你能看到宏观大局，看清各处到底在发生什么。

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:33.020
And as the adrenaline kicks in and you have to make, especially if you're in the hot seat, you have to make that decision as to whether or not you feel that you're going to make a 207. It is a rush.
随着肾上腺素飙升，你必须做出决定——特别是当你坐在风口浪尖上时，你必须决定自己是否觉得能在2点07分前完成。这真是一种冲劲。

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:41.060
It's a rush because you're up against the wall, the clock is ticking, and if you make the wrong decision, it's going to cost.
这是一种冲劲，因为你被逼入绝境，时钟在滴答作响，如果你做出错误的决定，就会付出代价。

00:02:45.640 --> 00:03:32.700
One thing that's interesting about the delivery business is that it isn't just people who know who are on the top of the whole operation like this guy who um thrive on the pressure and hit this kind of euphoria even you know even at the very bottom you meet people like this tabitha tate for example she stands in um a row of a dozen people who do what's called the secondary sort for belt two to say when you wander around fedex you're constantly constantly telling you things like okay here's the secondary sort and here's where the you know here's where the matrix begins here's the other matrix and you know they're all the names of the buildings are like you know the hub and the super hub and the mega hub and the ultra hub anyway so tabitha tate stands there and she has memorized 700 fedex destination codes and she stands by a bin of packages and she grabs one with her left hand
快递行业有趣的一点在于，不仅是那些身居高位、了解整个运营全局的人——比如这个享受压力并达到这种欣快状态的家伙——甚至在社会最底层，你也能遇到这样的人，比如塔比莎·泰特（Tabitha Tate）。她站在一排十来个人当中，做着所谓的2号传送带的二次分拣。如果你在联邦快递闲逛，他们会不停地、不停地向你介绍各种事情，比如“好，这是二次分拣，这里是矩阵开始的地方，这是另一个矩阵”，建筑物名字也叫什么“枢纽”、“超级枢纽”、“巨型枢纽”和“超级巨型枢纽”之类的。总之，塔比莎·泰特就站在那里，她背熟了700个联邦快递的目的地代码。她站在一堆包裹旁，左手抓起一个，

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:47.620
one with her right and she barely glances at them as she like pitches them into 10 different destination slots faster than than you would think is humanly possible so what do you think about while you're doing this i don't just really think about anything.
右手抓起一个，她甚至不怎么看它们，就像扔飞盘一样把它们扔进10个不同的目的地槽里，速度快得超乎人类的想象。所以你做这件事的时候在想什么？我其实什么都没想。

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:51.380
I just, it's like a flow.
我只是，这就像是一种心流。

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:53.660
Kind of like feeling.
有点像一种感觉。

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:54.880
You know what I'm saying?
你知道我在说什么吗？

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.160
You already know where it goes.
你心里已经知道它该去哪里。

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.400
You don't have to think about it, it just goes.
你不需要思考，它自然就发出去了。

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:06.560
And then I'm able to sort more than 47 packages per minute, which we're required to sort.
然后我能够每分钟分拣47个以上的包裹，这是公司对我们的要求。

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:07.580
How many per minute?
每分钟多少个？

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.200
47. How many do you sort?
47个。你每分钟分拣多少个？

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:15.560
60. And is it sort of a macho thing where you guys were like, you know, who's faster, who's slower?
60个。这有点像男子气概的较量吗？你们会比谁更快，谁更慢？

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:16.600
Yeah.
是的。

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:17.320
Really?
真的吗？

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:17.720
Yeah.
是的。

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.300
Sometimes we try to compete with each other.
有时候我们会互相比赛。

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:25.160
Welcome to WBEZ Chicago.
欢迎收听芝加哥WBEZ电台。

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:26.100
It's This American Life.
这里是《美国生活》。

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:26.700
I'm Ira Glass.
我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:28.220
Today on our program, deliveries.
今天我们的节目主题是：快递。

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:31.320
Act one, speed.
第一幕：速度。

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.460
A few words from somebody who has cut you off in in traffic and laughed.
听听某个在交通拥堵中强行超车还冷笑的人的心声。

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:44.200
Act two, Who You Meet, a short story by Junot Diaz about delivering pool tables.
第二幕：你遇到的人，这是一篇由朱诺·迪亚斯（Junot Diaz）创作的关于运送台球桌的短篇小说。

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:49.320
Act three, the world premiere of a new radio drama by David Sedaris.
第三幕：大卫·塞德里斯（David Sedaris）全新广播剧的世界首播。

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:50.980
It's kind of a radio experiment.
这算是一次广播实验。

00:04:51.040 --> 00:05:00.320
We gave him a sound effects record and this challenge, to create a radio play using only the effects on this one record and using all the effects on the record.
我们给了他一张音效唱片，并提出了这样一个挑战：创作一部广播剧，只能使用这张唱片里的音效，并且必须用光唱片里的所有音效。

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:02.700
We hear what he came up with.
让我们听听他创作出了什么。

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.740
Act four, Or check out the package on that guy.
第四幕：瞧瞧那家伙带的包裹。

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.040
An investigation into whether UPS men actually do have sex with their customers on their route.
一项关于UPS（联合包裹运送服务）快递员是否真的会与送货路线上的客户发生性关系的调查。

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:14.720
Act 5.
第五幕。

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:23.560
An unhappy customer gets a phone call from the CEO of Federal Express and still keeps his personal boycott going of the company.
一个不满的客户接到了联邦快递首席执行官的电话，但依然继续对该公司进行个人抵制。

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:25.080
Stay with us.
请继续收听。

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:32.920
Act 1.
第一幕。

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:33.740
on speed.
关于速度。

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.060
On the street, as a delivery worker, you know, you're mostly alone.
在街上，作为一个快递员，你知道，你大部分时间都是孤身一人。

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.960
You're moving fast from place to place.
你在各个地方之间快速穿梭。

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.240
And you spend a lot of time basically in your own head.
你花很多时间基本上都沉浸在自己的思绪里。

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:45.750
And some people, you know, play little games on the job.
你知道，有些人会在工作中玩一些小游戏。

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.340
They set little challenges for themselves.
他们为自己设定一些小挑战。

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:59.040
And just like there's a euphoria for the people who are under so much pressure at Federal Express in the middle of the night, there's this euphoria to moving through traffic and racing the clock to get your stuff where it's supposed to go.
就像半夜在联邦快递承受巨大压力的人会有一种欣快感一样，在车流中穿梭、与时间赛跑将货物送到指定地点的过程，也会带来这种欣快感。

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:02.200
Tony Starbucks delivered delivered produce in San Francisco.
托尼·斯塔克斯（Tony Starbucks）曾在旧金山送过农产品。

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:08.740
At his job, he used to wake up at 2.30 every morning, get stoned on the loading dock with the other drivers, and head out.
在他那份工作中，他以前每天早上2点30分醒来，和其他司机在装货区抽着大麻，然后出发。

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.400
Everybody had their own routes, and there were, you know, sort of like expected times that you would finish your routes in.
每个人都有自己的路线，你知道的，完成路线通常有预期的时间。

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:22.160
And part of the reasons why we had so many accidents, especially me, was because I was obsessed with doing my route faster than anybody else.
我们发生那么多事故的部分原因，尤其是对我来说，是因为我痴迷于比其他人更快地完成送货路线。

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:27.740
And I would literally, like, shave, like, I think I got it when I first started the route, I shaved, like, an hour off of, you know.
我简直会，比如，我记得刚开始跑这条路线时，我把时间缩短了，大概缩短了一个小时。

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.370
So instead of doing it in three hours, I would do it in two hours.
所以原本需要三个小时完成的活，我两个小时就能搞定。

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:41.400
But along the way, you know, I would wipe out people's cars, I wouldn't talk to the clients, I would drop off the potatoes, and they would try to engage me in a conversation, and all I could think about is, let me just get the hell out of here and, like, you know, get on my way.
但在这一过程中，你知道的，我会撞坏别人的车，不跟客户说话，放下土豆就走，他们想跟我搭话，而我满脑子想的只有：让我赶紧离开这鬼地方，上路吧。

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.060
The way I would shave the time is I ran a lot of red lights.
我缩短时间的方法就是闯了很多红灯。

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.840
I would drive 65 miles an hour down a city street.
我会在城市街道上以每小时65英里的速度狂飙。

00:06:56.150 --> 00:07:06.740
It got to the point, though, where I realized that after, whatever, a year of driving, that I was getting into increments of, like, one minute, 30 seconds.
不过后来发展到这种地步：在开车一年左右后，我发现自己缩短的时间只剩下区区一分钟、30秒这样的幅度。

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:13.820
I mean, literally, I would, like, drive 100 miles an hour into the loading bay, slam on the brakes, and look at the clock.
我是说，真的，我会以每小时100英里的速度冲进装货区，猛踩刹车，然后看一眼时钟。

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:42.960
I remember one day driving along 50 miles an hour down this street, and I see it's a two-lane, one-way street, and I see in the right-hand lane about two blocks ahead of me, like, just your nondescript white rental car just sort of going along and, like, can't decide if they want to go right or left.
我记得有一天，我以每小时50英里的速度沿着这条街开着，我看到这是一条双车道单行道，我发现在我前方大约两个街区处的右侧车道上，有一辆普普通通的白色租赁汽车慢吞吞地开着，好像拿不定主意是向右转还是向左转。

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:52.300
And two blocks away from them, I go, you know, if I really wanted to, I could slow down right now because I know they're going to do something really stupid.
离他们还有两个街区时，我想，如果我真的想的话，我现在可以减速，因为我知道他们肯定会干出什么非常蠢的事情。

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.640
But then there was this part of me that said, well, to hell with that.
但随后我心里又有一个声音说，算了吧。

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.680
I'm just, I'm on my route, I have to do what I have to do.
我正在赶路，我得做我该做的事。

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:02.180
And of course, as I got within like 20 feet of them, they decided to turn left from the right lane, right in front of me.
当然，当我距离他们只有大约20英尺时，他们决定从右侧车道左转，直接拐到了我前面。

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:08.400
I completely totaled their car, and my first reaction was to get out of the car, and I wanted to yell at them.
我彻底把他们的车撞报废了，而我的第一反应是下车，我想冲他们大吼大嚷。

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:16.040
It wasn't like, you know, you could have prevented this, you saw this coming, but it was like, you have now slowed me down, you've screwed up my whole day.
不是那种“你本可以避免这起事故，你早就看到它要发生”的语气，而是那种“你现在耽误了我的时间，你把我的整天都搞砸了”的愤怒。

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:29.900
And really, if it wasn't for this group of PG&amp;E workers, electrical workers working on the street out there, who saw this and somehow decided that I was in the right, because I guess it was like this blue-collar bonding thing.
真的，要不是当时有一群太平洋天然气与电力公司（PG&amp;E）的筑路电工在街上看到了这一幕，并且不知为何认定我是占理的一方——我猜这大概是因为某种蓝领阶层的抱团心理吧。

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:31.220
Like, he's a truck driver.
就像是：他是个卡车司机。

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:34.840
There's no way these people in this rented car, you know, can be right.
开着租来汽车的这帮人绝对不可能是对的。

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:39.420
I think I would have really gotten into trouble because I obviously was speeding like a maniac.
我觉得我当时肯定会惹上大麻烦，因为我显然是在像疯子一样超速行驶。

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:44.780
So, you know, I sort of like walked through my whole job that way, except the time I ran into the Bank of America building.
所以，你知道的，我基本上就是这样对待我的整个工作的，除了那次我直接撞进了美国银行大楼里。

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.800
You're in this very surreal world.
你身处这样一个非常超现实的世界里。

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.220
I mean, nobody else is up.
我是说，没有别人醒着。

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:56.760
You're bombing down the streets.
你在街道上飞驰。

00:08:57.610 --> 00:08:58.930
I think that's part of the problem.
我想这就是部分问题所在。

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:04.480
When all of a sudden people appear on the streets, you're like, look, I've been on the streets already for three hours.
当人们突然出现在街上时，你会觉得：听着，我在街上已经待了三个小时了。

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.640
Like, just get out of my way.
所以，赶紧让开。

00:09:13.440 --> 00:10:03.900
i remember one day um you you knew when the record was like you knew when the record was beatable because you would be halfway through your your your route and let's say you'd done it in 45 minutes and you knew that like if you just gave it a little extra energy that you could get through the day but i remember one day like okay i was halfway through my route and there was was this woman in front of me at this intersection, and the light turned yellow, and she like slowed down, and she really could have gone through the yellow light, and I could have like gone through, and it would have been perfect, and I, you know, had all the lights timed, and she slowed down, and I was sitting in my truck, and I lost it, and I lost it to the point where before the light turned green, I like let the truck, I like put it in neutral i was on a slight hill and i just sort of bumped her
我记得有一天，嗯，你知道什么时候记录是可以被打破的，因为当你跑到路线的一半时，比方说你只用了45分钟，你就知道，只要你再加把劲，就能熬过这一天。但我记得有一天，好吧，当时我正跑到路线的一半，在这个路口我前面有一位女士，交通灯变黄了，她却减速了，她其实完全可以闯过黄灯的，而我也可以跟着过去，那样就完美了，而且，你知道的，我把所有的红灯时间都算准了，可她却减速了。我坐在卡车里，情绪崩溃了，崩溃到在绿灯亮起之前，我竟然让卡车——我把它挂入空挡，当时正处于一个微小的坡道上——然后我就直接撞了她一下。

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:58.280
and then immediately the minute i bumped her it's like you know it's like this huge guilt washed over me and i'm like okay that was like that was violent like okay it's one thing to be screaming and yelling in your truck listening to like you know motorhead it's another thing to physically violate somebody like that so then i realized okay you know i crossed some line that you know uh i guess you just shouldn't across what basically happens the psychology i think of a delivery driver is that the job is so boring this i mean and it's stressful there's no more stressful job than driving in traffic especially in an urban environment.
然后就在我撞上她的那一瞬间，你知道的，那种巨大的负罪感瞬间向我涌来，我心想：好吧，那太暴力了，听着，在卡车里尖叫怒吼、听着像“摩托头”（Motorhead）乐队的音乐是一回事，对另一个人进行身体上的侵犯则是另一回事。于是我意识到，好吧，你知道的，我跨越了某种不该跨越的界限。快递员的心理本质上是怎样的呢？我认为，这项工作太无聊了，我是说，而且压力很大。没有比在交通中开车压力更大的工作了，尤其是在城市环境中。

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:04.160
And so then it all gets reduced to minutia.
于是，一切都被简化成了鸡毛蒜皮的小事。

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:08.580
It gets reduced to this guy who's in this little car ahead of you who, like, somehow becomes your archenemy.
一切都被简化成了前方开着小车的这个家伙，不知怎么的，他成了你势不两立的死敌。

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:18.960
And so it just creates an environment where you get very hostile and you just, you know, everybody is an inconvenience.
这就创造了一种环境，让你变得充满敌意，并且你会觉得，你知道的，每个人都是个累赘。

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.720
And all you want to do is get from point A to point B.
你唯一想做的事就是从A点到达B点。

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:37.850
Then there was the one part of the delivery job which was always, you know, there you are, you're screaming, you're yelling, or you're just like, you know, you're just having these outbursts.
然后，送货工作中总会有这么一部分时刻：你还在那儿，你大喊大叫，或者你只是，你知道的，你只是在情绪失控、大发雷霆。

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:41.620
And then there's the beautiful secretary walking to work with the short skirt.
然后你会看到穿着短裙的美丽女秘书正步行去上班。

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:55.520
And it would be like this, like all the noise would stop and you'd just have like this momentary like calm and then all of a sudden she would disappear from view and then you were like thrust right back into, you know, the craziness of traffic.
那种感觉就像所有的喧嚣瞬间停止，你突然获得了一种短暂的平静，紧接着她又从视野中消失了，然后你又被猛烈地拉回了交通的疯狂之中。

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.200
and trying to get to where you're going.
并且急着赶往你要去的地方。

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:00.480
That would always save the day, actually.
这总能打破僵局，真的。

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:11.920
Tony Starbucks was interviewed by Paul Tuff and Deirdre Dolan in New York.
托尼·斯塔克斯在纽约接受了保罗·塔夫（Paul Tuff）和戴德丽·多兰（Deirdre Dolan）的采访。

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:42.260
the paper boy he comes each day our paper in his hand he throws the paper towards our door and on our fortune lands on sunny days on rainy days our paper boy comes by he never never misses us Because if he did, I'd cry.
送报童，他每天都来，手里拿着我们的报纸。他把报纸扔向我们的门前，落在我们的财富之上。在阳光灿烂的日子里，在下雨的日子里，送报童都会来。他从来、从来不会漏掉我们，因为如果他漏掉了，我会哭的。

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:46.080
Now, why don't we all try it together?
现在，为什么我们不一起试试呢？

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:46.720
Ready?
准备好了吗？

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:48.900
One, two, three.
一，二，三。

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.060
The paper boy, he comes each day.
送报童，他每天都来。

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:53.560
Act two, who you meet.
第二幕：你遇到的人。

00:12:54.820 --> 00:13:06.120
There's the game that you play in your head when you work, and there are also the worlds that your job takes you into as you go house to house, office to office, like a spy, having little encounters with people along the way as a delivery person.
工作时你在脑海中玩的游戏是一回事，而你的工作带你深入的世界则是另一回事——你挨家挨户、挨个办公室地走着，就像一个间谍，作为一名快递员，沿途与人们进行着小小的接触。

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:11.900
This short story is by Junot Diaz about guys who deliver gold crowns and bristles and schmalkies.
这篇短篇小说由朱诺·迪亚斯（Junot Diaz）创作，讲述了运送金冠、刷子和施茅基（schmalkies）的人。

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:13.100
Pool tables.
也就是台球桌。

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:18.040
A warning before we start that some of the language in this story might not be suitable for younger listeners.
开始之前先作个警告：本故事中的某些语言可能不适合年轻听众收听。

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:23.900
The first time we try to deliver the gold crown, the lights are on in the house, but no one lets us in.
我们第一次去送那顶金冠时，屋里的灯亮着，但没有人给我们开门。

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:30.180
I bang on the front door, and Wayne hits the back, and I can hear our double drum shaking the windows.
我用力敲前门，韦恩敲后门，我能听到我们抬的双层鼓声震动着窗户。

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.160
Right then, I have this feeling that someone is inside, laughing at us.
就在那时，我有一种感觉，里面有人在嘲笑我们。

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.420
This guy better have a good excuse, Wayne says.
这家伙最好有个好借口，韦恩说。

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:40.040
This is bulls**t.
这太扯淡了。

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:44.680
You're telling me, I say, but Wayne's the one who takes his job too seriously.
可不是嘛，我说，不过韦恩是那种把工作看得太重的人。

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:48.420
He pounds some more on the door, his face jiggling.
他又猛砸了几下门，脸上的肉都在抖动。

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.840
A couple of times he raps on the windows, tries squinting through the curtains.
他还在窗户上敲了几下，试着透过窗帘往里眯着眼睛看。

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:55.680
I take a more philosophical approach.
我采取了一种比较超然的态度。

00:13:56.580 --> 00:14:02.340
I walk over to the ditch that's been cut next to the road, a drainage pipe half-filled with water, and sit down.
我走到路边挖好的水沟旁，那是一根半满着水的排水管，然后坐了下来。

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:10.000
I smoke and watch a mama duck and her three ducklings scavenge the grassy bank and then float downstream like they're on the same string.
我抽着烟，看着一只母鸭和它的三只小鸭子在长满草的河岸上觅食，然后像串在同一根绳子上似地顺流而下。

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.700
Beautiful, I say, but Wayne doesn't hear.
真美啊，我说，但韦恩根本没听见。

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:16.380
He's banging on the door with a staple gun.
他正用钉枪猛砸着门。

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:29.380
At nine, Wayne picks me up at the showroom, and by then I have our route planned out.
九点钟的时候，韦恩来展厅接我，那时候我已经规划好了我们的送货路线。

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:34.200
The order form tells me everything I need to know about the customers we'll be dealing with that day.
订单上的信息告诉我一切需要了解的关于那天我们要打交道的客户情况。

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:42.650
If someone is just getting a 52-inch card table delivered, then you know they aren't going to give you too much of a hassle, but they also aren't going to tip.
如果有人只是订购一张52英寸的纸牌桌，那你就会知道他们不会给你带来太多麻烦，但他们也绝不会给小费。

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:46.160
Those are your Spotswood, Sayreville, and Perth Amboy deliveries.
那些就是你们去斯波茨伍德、塞雷维尔和珀斯安博伊的订单。

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:51.600
The pool tables go north to the rich suburbs, Livingston, Ridgewood, Bedminster.
台球桌则是往北送到富人区，利文斯顿、里奇伍德、贝德明斯特。

00:14:53.480 --> 00:15:03.580
You should see our customers, doctors, diplomats, surgeons, presidents of universities, Ladies in slacks and silk tops who sport thin watches you could trade in for a car.
你应该看看我们的客户，医生、外交官、外科医生、大学校长，穿着宽松裤和丝绸上衣、戴着可以换辆车的精美手表的女士们。

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:09.900
Most of them prepare for us by laying down a path of yesterday's Washington Post from the front door to the game room.
他们大多数人为我们做准备的方式，是从前门到游戏室铺上一条昨天的《华盛顿邮报》。

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:12.060
I make them pick it all up.
我让他们把这些全捡起来。

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:15.100
I say, carajo, what if we slip?
我说，混蛋，如果我们滑倒了怎么办？

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:18.220
Do you know what 200 pounds of slate could do to a floor?
你知道200磅重的石板会对地板造成什么损害吗？

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:21.980
The threat of property damage puts the chop-chop in their step.
财产受损的威胁让他们动作麻利了起来。

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:22.180
step.
麻利起来。

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:30.790
Sometimes the customer has to jet to the store for cat food or a newspaper while we're in the middle of a job.
有时，当我们正干到一半时，客户不得不冲去商店买猫粮或报纸。

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.380
I'm sure you'll be all right, they say.
我相信你们能搞定的，他们会这么说。

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.420
They never sound too sure.
他们听起来从来都不是那么确定。

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:38.960
Of course, I say, just show us where the silver's at.
当然，我说，只要带我们看看银器放在哪儿就行。

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:51.240
The customer's ha-ha and we ha-ha, and then they agonize over leaving, linger by the front door, trying to memorize everything they own, as if if they don't know where to find us, who we work for.
客户哈哈大笑，我们也哈哈大笑，然后他们为离开而纠结，在前门徘徊，试图记住他们拥有的每一件东西，仿佛如果他们不知道去哪里找我们，不知道我们为谁效劳似的。

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:02.540
Once they're gone, I don't have to worry about anyone bothering me.
一旦他们离开，我就不用担心任何人来打扰我了。

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:10.020
I put down the ratchet, crack my knuckles, and explore, usually while Wayne is smoothing out the felt and doesn't need help.
我放下棘轮扳手，活动了一下手指关节，开始四处探索，通常是在韦恩抚平呢绒台布、不需要帮忙的时候。

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:13.860
I take cookies from the kitchen, razors from the bathroom cabinets.
我从厨房拿饼干，从浴室柜里拿剃须刀。

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:16.660
Some of these houses have 20, 30 rooms.
其中有些房子有20间、30间房。

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:21.260
On the ride back, I try to figure out how much loot it would take to fill up all that space.
在回去的路上，我试图盘算一下，得用多少战利品才能填满所有这些空间。

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:31.320
I've been caught roaming around plenty of times, but you'd be surprised how quickly someone believes you're looking for the bathroom if you don't jump when you're discovered, if you just say hi.
我被抓到到处闲逛过很多次，但你会惊讶于，如果你在被发现时没有吓得跳起来，只是简单地打个招呼，别人是多么容易相信你是在找洗手间。

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:35.460
After the paperwork's been signed, I have a decision to make.
在签署完文书工作后，我面临着一个决定。

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:40.120
If the customer has been good and tipped well, we call it even and leave.
如果客户表现不错且给的小费很丰厚，我们就两清并离开。

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:46.080
If the customer has been an ass, maybe they yelled, maybe they let their kids throw golf balls at us.
如果客户是个混蛋，也许他们大喊大叫，也许他们任由自己的孩子拿高尔夫球砸我们。

00:16:46.230 --> 00:17:31.840
I ask for the bathroom Wayne will pretend that he hasn't seen this before excuse me, I'll say I let them show me the way to the bathroom usually I already know and once the door is shut I cram bubble bath drops into my pockets and throw fist-sized wads of toilet paper into the toilet I take a dump if I can and leave that for them most of the time Wayne and I work well together he's the driver and the money man and I do the lifting and handle the ass tonight we're on our way to Lawrenceville and he wants to talk to me about Charlene one of the showroom girls I haven't wanted to talk about women in months not since the a girlfriend.
我要求去洗手间，韦恩会假装他从没见过这出戏，不好意思，我会说，我让他们带我去洗手间，通常我早就知道了，一旦门关上，我就把泡泡浴球塞进衣兜里，把拳头大小的卫生纸团扔进马桶里，如果可以的话我还会拉上一堆留给他们。大多数时候我和韦恩合作得很愉快，他是司机和管钱的人，而我负责搬运和应付这些混蛋。今晚我们在去劳伦斯维尔的路上，他想跟我聊聊夏琳，展厅里的女孩之一。我已经好几个月不想谈论女人了，自从上一个女朋友之后。

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:34.800
I really want a piler, he tells me.
我真的很想泡她，他告诉我。

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:36.380
Maybe I'm one of the Madisons.
也许我是其中一个麦迪逊。

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:41.960
Man, I say, cutting my eyes towards him, don't you have a wife or something?
老兄，我说，斜眼瞥了他一下，你难道没有老婆什么的吗？

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:43.560
He gets quiet.
他安静了下来。

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:49.540
Twice this year, Wayne's cheated on his wife, and I've heard it all, the before and the after.
今年已经有两次了，韦恩背着他老婆偷吃，而我都听遍了，前前后后的所有事。

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:52.940
The last time his wife nearly tossed his ass out to the dogs.
上次他老婆差点把他这混蛋扫地出门，扔给狗吃。

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:55.460
Neither of the women seemed worth it to me.
在我看来，这两个女人都不值得他这么做。

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:57.780
One of them was even younger than Charlene.
其中一个甚至比夏琳还要年轻。

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:01.400
Wayne can be a moody guy, and this is one of of those nights.
韦恩是个喜怒无常的人，今晚就是那种日子。

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:08.420
He slouches in the driver's seat and swerves through traffic, riding other people's bumpers like I've told him not to do.
他瘫坐在驾驶座上，在车流中左穿右插，像我警告过他别做的那样，紧贴着别人的车尾行驶。

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:18.400
I don't need a collision or a four-hour silent treatment, so I try to forget that I think his wife is good people and ask him if Charlene's given him any signals.
我可不想发生车祸或者遭遇四个小时的冷暴力，所以我尽量不去想我认为他老婆是个好人这件事，而是问他夏琳有没有给他释放什么信号。

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:20.840
He slows the truck down.
他把卡车车速慢了下来。

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:23.980
Signals like you wouldn't believe, he says.
那信号简直让你难以置信，他说。

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:40.600
The second time we bring the gold crown, the heavy curtain next to the door swings up like a Spanish fan.
我们第二次把金冠搬去时，门旁边的厚窗帘像西班牙折扇一样哗啦一声拉开了。

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:44.780
A woman stares at me, and wanes too busy knocking to see.
一个女人盯着我看，而韦恩正忙着敲门，什么也没看到。

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:46.720
Muñeca, I say.
美女，我说。

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:52.560
She's black and unsmiling, and then the curtain drops between us, a whisper on the glass.
她是黑人，没有笑，接着窗帘在我们之间落下，像玻璃上的轻声低语。

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:57.840
She had on a T-shirt that said, No Problem, and didn't look like she owned the place.
她穿着一件印有“没问题”字样的T恤，看起来不像是这里的主人。

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:04.700
She looked more like the help, and couldn't have been older than 20, and from the thinness of her face I pictured the rest of her skinny.
她看起来更像是帮佣，年纪绝对不会超过20岁，从她消瘦的脸庞，我能想象出她身上其他地方也很苗条。

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:11.840
We stared at each other for a second at the most, not enough for me to notice the shape of her ears or if her lips were chapped.
我们顶多互相对视了一秒钟，时间短得不足以让我注意到她耳朵的形状，或者她的嘴唇是否干裂。

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:14.020
I've fallen in love on less.
我曾因更少的触动而坠入爱河。

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:20.360
Later in the truck, on the way back to the showroom, Wayne mutters, This guy is dead.
后来在卡车上，在回展厅的路上，韦恩嘟囔着说：这家伙死定了。

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:21.520
I mean it.
我是认真的。

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:32.220
The girlfriend calls sometimes, but not often.
前女友有时会打电话，但并不频繁。

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:37.100
She has found herself a new boyfriend, some sangano who works at a record store.
她自己找了个新男友，一个在唱片店工作的笨蛋。

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:44.000
Dan is his name, and the way she says it, so painfully gringo, makes the corner of my eyes narrow.
丹是他的名字，她叫这个名字时那种痛苦的美国腔，让我眼角微微眯了起来。

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.420
The last time I saw her in person was in Hoboken.
我最后一次见到她本人是在霍博肯。

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:55.040
She was with Dan, and hadn't yet told me about him, and hurried across the street in her high clogs to avoid me and my boys.
她和丹在一起，当时还没跟我提过他，她穿着厚底高跟鞋急忙穿过街道，为了避开我和我的兄弟们。

00:19:55.680 --> 00:20:24.380
a month before the sangano i went to her house a friend visiting a friend and her parents asked me how business was as if i balanced the books or something business is outstanding i said that's really wonderful to hear the father said you betcha he asked me to help him mow his lawn and while we were dribbling gas into the tank he offered me a job a real one that you can build on Utilities, he said, is nothing to be ashamed of.
就在那个笨蛋出现前一个月，我去过她家，像个朋友去拜访朋友，她的父母问我生意怎么样，就好像我负责管账或者别的什么。生意非常好，我说。听到这个真高兴，父亲说。可不是嘛。他邀请我帮他修剪草坪，当我们把汽油倒进油箱时，他问我想不想找份工作，那种可以长久干下去的正经工作。公用事业，他说，这没什么好丢脸的。

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:38.140
The boss nearly kicked our asses over the gold crown.
老板差点因为金冠的事把我们痛骂一顿。

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:43.560
The customer, an a**hole named Pruitt, called up crazy, said we were delinquent.
那个客户，一个叫普鲁特的混蛋，气急败坏地打来电话，说我们擅离职守。

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:45.940
That's how the boss put it, delinquent.
老板就是这么说的，擅离职守。

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:50.680
We knew that's what the customer called us because the boss doesn't use words like that.
我们知道那是客户对我们的称呼，因为老板平时根本不用这种词。

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:52.600
Look, boss, I said.
听着，老板，我说。

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:54.160
We knocked like crazy.
我们疯狂地敲门了。

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:57.700
I mean, we knocked like federal marshals, like Paul Bunyan.
我是说，我们敲得像联邦法警一样，像保罗·班扬一样。

00:20:58.200 --> 00:20:59.780
The boss wasn't having it.
老板根本听不进去。

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:03.600
He tore us for a good two minutes and then dismissed us.
他把我们狠狠训了整整两分钟，然后打发我们走了。

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:13.510
For most of the night, I didn't think I had a job, so I hit the bars, fantasizing that I would bump into this cabron out with that black woman while me and my boys were cranked.
大半个晚上，我都觉得自己失业了，于是我去泡酒吧，幻想着能撞见这个混蛋带着那个黑人女人出来，而我和我的兄弟们已经喝高了。

00:21:14.060 --> 00:21:16.840
But the next morning, Wayne came by with the gold crown again.
但第二天早上，韦恩又带着金冠过来了。

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:18.690
Both of us had hangovers.
我们俩都宿醉未醒。

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:20.940
One more time, he said.
再来一次，他说。

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:23.320
An extra delivery, no overtime.
多送一趟，没有加班费。

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:27.580
We hammered on the door for ten minutes, but no one answered.
我们砸了十分钟的门，但没人应答。

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:33.000
I jimmied with the window and the back door, and I could have sworn I heard her behind the patio door.
我撬了撬窗户和后门，我敢发誓我听到她在天井门后面。

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:35.440
I knocked hard and heard footsteps.
我用力敲门，听到了脚步声。

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:41.380
We called the boss and told him what was what, and the boss called the house, but no one answered.
我们给老板打电话说明了情况，老板给那栋房子打了电话，但没有人接。

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:44.900
Okay, the boss said, get those card tables done.
好吧，老板说，去把那些纸牌桌搞定。

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:51.820
That night, as we lined up the next day's paperwork, work, we got a call from Pruitt and he didn't use the word delinquent.
那天晚上，当我们整理第二天的文书工作时，我们接到了普鲁特的电话，他没有再用“擅离职守”这个词。

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:56.920
Pruitt said he was contrite and determined and asked us to come again.
普鲁特说他感到很后悔也很坚决，并请我们再去一次。

00:21:57.320 --> 00:21:59.120
His maid was sure to let us in.
他的女佣一定会让我们进去的。

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:08.220
We park in front of Pruitt's house and bang on the door.
我们把车停在普鲁特家门前，开始砸门。

00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:11.800
I give Wayne a hard look when I see no car in the garage.
当我看到车库里没有车时，我严厉地看了韦恩一眼。

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:15.240
Yes, I hear a voice inside say.
来了，我听到里面有个声音说。

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:18.100
We're the delivery guys, I yell.
我们是送货的，我大喊。

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:21.980
A bolt slides, a lock turns, the door opens.
插销滑动，门锁转动，门开了。

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:28.420
She stands in our way, wearing black shorts and a gloss of red on her lips, and I'm sweating.
她挡在我们面前，穿着黑色短裤，嘴唇上涂着红色的唇彩，我直冒汗。

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:30.380
Come in, yes?
进来，好吗？

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:33.340
She stands back from the door, holding it open.
她从门口退开，把门敞开着。

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:35.920
Sounds like Spanish, Wayne says.
听起来像是西班牙语，韦恩说。

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:39.080
No s**t, I say, switching over.
废话，我说，切换成西班牙语。

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:40.880
Do you remember me?
你还记得我吗？

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:42.680
No, she says.
不记得，她说。

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:44.540
I look over at Wayne.
我看向韦恩。

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:46.080
Can you believe this?
你敢信吗？

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:48.020
I can believe anything, kid.
我什么都信，老弟。

00:22:49.280 --> 00:22:50.260
You heard us, didn't you?
你听到我们敲门了吧？

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:52.080
The other day, that was you.
前几天，那就是你。

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:54.660
She shrugs and opens the door wider.
她耸耸肩，把门推得更开。

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:57.600
You better tell her to prop that with a chair.
你最好叫她拿把椅子把门撑住。

00:22:58.400 --> 00:22:59.920
Wayne heads back to unlock the truck.
韦恩走回车去开卡车锁。

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:02.140
You hold that door, I say.
你给我把门扶好，我说。

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:28.900
she stays in the kitchen while we work i can hear her humming wayne shaking his right hand like he scalded his fingertips yeah she's fine she has her back to me her hand stirring around in a full sink when i walk in i try to sound conciliatory you're from the city a nod where whereabout?
她留在厨房里，我们在干活时我能听到她在哼歌，韦恩正甩着他的右手，就像烫伤了指尖一样。好吧，她很好。当我走进去时，她背对著我，双手在装满水的水槽里搅拌着，我尽量让语气听起来温和一些：你是从城里来的？她点点头。哪里？华盛顿高地。多米尼加人，我说。基斯奎亚人。她点点头。哪条街？我不知道地址，她说。我写下来了。我妈妈和兄弟们都住在那里。我是多米尼加人，我说。你看起来不像。我拿了一杯水。我们俩都看着泥泞的草坪。她说，我没开门是因为我想让他生气。让谁生气？我想离开这里，她说，离开这里。我付你车费。我看不行，我说。你不是来自纽约吗？不是。那你为什么问地址？因为我附近有家人。这会有那么大问题吗？我用英语说，她应该让她老板带她去，但她茫然地看着我。

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:30.600
Washington Heights.
我切换回西班牙语。

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:32.700
Dominicana, I say.
他是个混蛋，她突然生气地说。

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:33.920
Quiqueana.
我放下杯子，走到她旁边去洗杯子。

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:35.800
She nods.
她和我一样高，身上有液体洗涤剂的香味，脖子上长着几颗细小美丽的痣。

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:36.860
What street?
给我，她说，伸出手来，但我自己洗完，回到了书房。

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:39.500
I don't know the address, she says.
你知道她想让我们做什么吗？我对韦恩说。

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:40.920
I have it written down.
韦恩正用牧田电钻把螺栓钉入石板中。

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:43.080
My mother and my brothers live there.
你不能这么做，他说。

00:23:44.440 --> 00:23:45.400
I'm Dominican, I say.
为什么不能？

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:47.020
You don't look it.
老弟，我们得把这活干完。

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:49.040
I get a glass of water.
我一转眼就回来。

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:51.240
We're both staring out at the muddy lawn.
快去快回，速去速回。

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:55.660
She says, I didn't answer the door because I wanted to make him mad.
老弟，他缓缓站起身来。

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:57.160
Make who mad?
他年纪比我大将近一倍。

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:20.880
I want to get out of here she says out of here i'll pay you for a ride i don't think so i say aren't you from nueva york no then why did you ask the address why i have family near there would it be that big of a problem i say in english that she should have her boss bring her but she stares at me blankly.
我走到窗前向外望去。

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:22.440
I switch over.
新的银杏树在车道旁排成一排。

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:25.920
